    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1420">

                <pb facs="1378" n="1368" xml:id="pg1368"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">026 China/6–1349</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Acting Secretary of
                            State of a Conversation With President Truman
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1949-06-13">June 13, 1949</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The President would like the White Paper on China sent to him as early as
                    possible and has said he would like to read it all. He considers its issuance as
                    one of our most important actions for some time to come. He asked if General
                    Marshall had seen it and I told him he had read the chapter relating to his own
                        activities.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d1420-f1"/> The President indicated that he
                    thought it important that General Marshall read it in its entirety prior to its
                    issuance. He also indicated that he considered it important that Secretary
                    Acheson have an opportunity to read it, if possible.<anchor n="10"
                        xml:id="d1420-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">James</hi> E. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Webb</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1420-f1" n="9" xml:id="d1420-f1-content">
                <p>General of the Army George C. Marshall was Personal Representative of President
                    Truman in China, December 1945–January 1947. For correspondence on his mission,
                    see <ref target="frus1945v07#pg_745">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1945, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">vii</hi>, pp. 745</ref> ff., and <ref
                        target="frus1946v09">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, 1946, vols. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >ix</hi>
                    </ref> and <ref target="frus1946v10">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">x</hi>
                    </ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1420-f2" n="10" xml:id="d1420-f2-content">
                <p>Mr. Acheson was in Paris attending a meeting of the Council of Foreign Ministers,
                    May 23–June 20, 1949.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
